Ah.  That helps a lot.  Especially since it's a limit you
only run into when modifying the game -- end users
would typically never encounter it.

> You know how hard resp. annoying it is in C to do something with
> dynamic memory allocation?

Hard, annoying, and extremely bug-prone, yes, I know.

(Something like 90% of all security patches are due
to this and closely related issues, e.g., the lack of
proper sanity checking variables are incremented
or otherwise assigned new values, unless the
programmer of each individual application writes
in code to do the checking.  Using C made sense
when NetHack was written, because back then
computer resources were much more expensive
and scarce relative to programmer time; it would
NOT make any sense for a new project now, even
for a game, where security is less significant than
for almost all other applications.  Of course,
changing the implementation language now would
mean a complete rewrite, which I do NOT suggest.)

I wasn't thinking of going dynamic.  I was just thinking,
if computers back when the last stable NetHack was
released could handle 200, and I were increasing the
limit to solve a problem where someone had run into the
limit, I'd have made the new limit at least a thousand,
probably several times that.

But the other UnNetHack changes you mention above
are arguably even better or at least mitigate the problem
rather significantly.

> If you don't and are used to higher-level languages

I'm definitely used to higher-level languages, where
dynamic allocation (and freeing) of memory is handled
much more automatically, but I'm familiar with low-level
languages in principle.  My C is rustier than the spare
dagger you keep around especially for killing Rs, but
just because I don't deal with linked lists and pointer
arithmetic and all that jazz on a regular basis doesn't
mean I don't know they exist.

